Number = {8097},
Month = {JAN 15},
Abstract = {Most human secretory pathway proteins are N-glycosylated by
   oligosaccharyltransferase (OST) complexes as they enter the endoplasmic
   reticulum (ER)1, 2-3. Recent work revealed a substrate-assisted
   mechanism by which N-glycosylation of the chaperone glucose-regulated
   protein 94 (GRP94) is regulated to control cell surface receptor
   signalling4. Here we report the structure of a natively isolated GRP94
   folding intermediate tethered to a specialized CCDC134-bound translocon.
   Together with functional analysis, the data reveal how a conserved
   N-terminal extension in GRP94 inhibits OST-A and how structural
   rearrangements within the translocon shield the tethered nascent chain
   from inappropriate OST-B glycosylation. These interactions depend on a
   hydrophobic CCDC134 groove, which recognizes a non-native conformation
   of nascent GRP94. Our results define a mechanism of regulated
   N-glycosylation and illustrate how the nascent chain remodels the
   translocon to facilitate its own biogenesis.},
